# Names the corpus resolves, appended to /etc/hosts by the verifier's test.sh.
#
# Docker writes /etc/hosts when the container starts, so these cannot be baked
# into the image: a RUN line that edits /etc/hosts edits a file that is replaced
# before anything runs.
#
# `dual` is the interesting one. Both of its addresses answer, so which family a
# program ends up on is the program's decision and not the resolver's -- which is
# the whole question this migration asks.
#
# `v6only` is not what its name suggests to an IPv4 caller, and this is worth
# knowing before reading anything that uses it. glibc treats `::1` in this file
# as also answering an `AF_INET` query for the same name, by substituting
# `127.0.0.1`:
#
#     getent ahostsv4 v6only.test   ->  127.0.0.1
#
# It is specific to the loopback address -- a name mapped to `2001:db8::77` gets
# EAI_NONAME from an `AF_INET` query, as expected -- and it does not happen for
# `AF_UNSPEC`, which returns `::1` alone. So the name still separates the two
# behaviours, but not in the way it looks like it does: a caller that asks for
# IPv4 is handed a *different address* and connects to a *different socket*,
# rather than failing to resolve. Anything scored against `v6only.test` has to
# put the server on `::1` alone for the distinction to be visible.
::1             v6only.test
127.0.0.1       v4only.test
::1             dual.test
127.0.0.1       dual.test
# `multi` has five addresses and only the first three of them are on this host,
# so whether a connection happens at all depends on the caller walking the list
# rather than stopping at the head of it. The third is bindable but unbound, so
# it is reachable only by a scenario that puts a server there on purpose.
#
# Every unusable address here is one that *refuses immediately*: the
# documentation prefix has no IPv6 route out of this container and answers
# ENETUNREACH, and the unbound loopback address answers ECONNREFUSED. An address
# in a private or public IPv4 block would instead be routed at the default
# gateway and time out, which would turn "the caller walked the list" into "the
# caller waited", and the time it waited is a property of the host.
::1             multi.test
127.0.0.1       multi.test
2001:db8::1     multi.test
2001:db8::2     multi.test
127.99.99.1     multi.test
# A name with no address of the other family, in each direction. `v6only` above
# is the v6 half; this is a second v4-only name so a scenario can use one for
# the client and one for the server without either being the same name twice.
127.0.0.1       legacy.test
# A second deep name, with a different list shape. In the order the resolver
# puts them:
#
#     ::1, 127.0.0.1, 127.99.99.2, 127.99.99.3, 127.99.99.4, 2001:db8::3
#
# -- the same sort as `multi`, for the same reasons: the IPv6 loopback first,
# then the IPv4 loopback addresses with the one the host actually carries ahead
# of the rest of 127/8, then the documentation prefix last because it has no
# route.
#
# Three things about it are not true of `multi`. It is longer, and its usable
# addresses are further in: 127.99.99.2 is third and 127.99.99.4 is fifth, so
# reaching the second of them means crossing four refusals rather than two, and
# three of those four in a row within one family rather than one per family.
# And it has *two* addresses that can carry a server rather than one, at
# different depths in the same list, which is the whole of what makes "which
# address did the walk stop at" a question that can be put from outside: put a
# server on each and the answer names one of them. Neither is ever at the head
# of the list, and there is an address after both of them, so a caller that
# takes the resolver's first answer and a caller that runs the list to the end
# both land nowhere.
#
# Like `multi` it ends on an address with no route, and `dual` above ends on one
# that refuses. That pairing is what makes "if none of them connects, the
# failure reported is the last one's" answerable without recording any message
# text: on this name the first address refuses and the last is unreachable, so
# the *kind* of failure says how far the caller got, and on `dual` every address
# refuses, so the same word comes back however far it got. Every address here
# still fails immediately -- none of them is routed at a gateway and none of
# them can make the caller wait.
::1             deep-tail.test
127.0.0.1       deep-tail.test
127.99.99.2     deep-tail.test
127.99.99.3     deep-tail.test
127.99.99.4     deep-tail.test
2001:db8::3     deep-tail.test
